  "slug": "image-diffractive-d927",
  "summary": "Recent preprints extend physics-informed learning to diffractive design, diffraction tomography and high-harmonic spectroscopy analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the embedding of explicit physics into learned models for optical inverse problems. MSWNets couple fully connected networks with Rayleigh-Sommerfeld propagation in a PINN-inspired formulation to design broadband diffractive optics. Gradient optical diffraction tomography uses physics-informed deep learning, with a ResNet, to estimate the missing amplitude gradient, extending gradient-based tomography to amplitude retrieval. Across these two diffractive items, what is new is that propagation physics is built into the learning or estimation step itself. The AI-assisted topological high-harmonic study retrieves anisotropic nonlinear responses and crystal symmetries of atomically thin materials; it shares only the learning-for-inference element and addresses a different nonlinear light-matter regime.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
